Web Server Log Analysis & Incident Response (Windows CLI)
📌 Overview
This project demonstrates an incident response procedure using native Windows Command Line utilities (CMD) to analyze web server logs (access.log). The primary objective was to investigate suspicious traffic, identify potential attack vectors (SQL Injection and Brute-Force login attempts), isolate malicious entries, and package digital evidence for forensic auditing.
🛠️ Tools & Technologies
OS Environment: Windows Command Prompt (CMD)
Log Parsing: findstr, type
Evidence Archiving: tar (Gzip compression)
Data Handling: Output redirection (>, ())
🔍 Incident Scenario
A client reported unauthorized access attempts and potential application errors on their web server. The security team was tasked with examining the HTTP access log (access_web.log) to answer three key investigation questions:
Were there any SQL Injection (SQLi) attack attempts?
Which IP address attempted a Brute-Force attack against the administrative login panel?
Can the relevant evidence be packaged securely for senior auditors?
📝 Step-by-Step Execution
Step 1: Environment Setup & Log Simulation
Simulated the web server access log using Windows CMD text piping to generate sample HTTP request records:
(
echo 192.168.1.10 - - [30/Sep/2026:10:00:01] "GET /index.html HTTP/1.1" 200 1420
echo 45.33.32.156 - - [30/Sep/2026:10:01:12] "GET /admin/login.php HTTP/1.1" 401 512
echo 45.33.32.156 - - [30/Sep/2026:10:01:15] "POST /admin/login.php HTTP/1.1" 401 512
echo 45.33.32.156 - - [30/Sep/2026:10:01:20] "POST /admin/login.php HTTP/1.1" 200 4200
echo 192.168.1.15 - - [30/Sep/2026:10:02:00] "GET /products.php?id=1' UNION SELECT NULL-- HTTP/1.1" 500 230
echo 45.33.32.156 - - [30/Sep/2026:10:03:45] "GET /admin/dashboard.php HTTP/1.1" 200 8900
) > access_web.log


Step 2: SQL Injection Identification
Filtered the log file for common SQL Injection keywords (UNION):
findstr "UNION" access_web.log


Output:
192.168.1.15 - - [30/Sep/2026:10:02:00] "GET /products.php?id=1' UNION SELECT NULL-- HTTP/1.1" 500 230


Finding: IP 192.168.1.15 attempted an inline SQL Injection attack on the id parameter, resulting in an HTTP 500 (Internal Server Error) code.
Step 3: Brute-Force & Unauthorized Access Isolation
Extracted all requests returning HTTP status code 401 (Unauthorized) to isolate failed authentication attempts into a separate report:
findstr "401" access_web.log > sospechosos.txt
type sospechosos.txt


Output (sospechosos.txt):
45.33.32.156 - - [30/Sep/2026:10:01:12] "GET /admin/login.php HTTP/1.1" 401 512
45.33.32.156 - - [30/Sep/2026:10:01:15] "POST /admin/login.php HTTP/1.1" 401 512


Finding: IP 45.33.32.156 performed consecutive unauthorized access attempts on /admin/login.php before gaining successful access (HTTP 200).
Step 4: Evidence Archiving
Packaged both the original log and the isolated suspicious activity report into a compressed .tar.gz archive for reporting:
tar -czvf evidencia_incidente.tar.gz access_web.log sospechosos.txt


